<Layout
  title="User Guide & Documentation — Limitcode Pro for Sublime Text"
  description="Complete user guide for Limitcode Pro in Sublime Text: installation, connecting AI providers, using the chat, passing code context, reviewing diffs, modes, permissions, Agent Skills, MCP servers, and shortcuts."
  canonicalPath="/docs/"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": faq.map((item) => ({
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": item.q,
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": item.a },
    })),
  })} />

  <Nav />

  <main class="docs-page">
    <!-- Top Header -->
    <header class="docs-hero">
      <div class="container">
        <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span>User Manual &amp; Reference</span>
        <h1>How to use Limitcode Pro.</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          Everything you need to install Limitcode in Sublime Text, connect your preferred AI models,
          collaborate on real codebases, review diffs safely, and customize the agent for your workflow.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Main 2-column Documentation Layout -->
    <div class="container docs-layout">
      <!-- Sticky Left Sidebar Navigation -->
      <aside class="docs-sidebar" aria-label="Documentation navigation">
        <div class="sidebar-inner">
          {navGroups.map((group) => (
            <div class="sidebar-group">
              <span class="sidebar-heading">{group.group}</span>
              <ul>
                {group.items.map((item) => (
                  <li>
                    <a href={`#${item.id}`} data-docs-link>{item.label}</a>
                  </li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            </div>
          ))}
          <div class="sidebar-cta">
            <p>Need Limitcode Pro?</p>
            <a class="btn btn-primary btn-sm" href="/#pricing">Get Pro — $29 →</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </aside>

      <!-- Right Documentation Content -->
      <div class="docs-content">

        <!-- SECTION 1: OVERVIEW -->
        <section id="overview" class="doc-block">
          <span class="doc-kicker">Getting Started</span>
          <h2>What Limitcode Does</h2>
          <p>
            Limitcode Pro is an autonomous AI coding assistant that lives directly inside <strong>Sublime Text 4</strong> (builds 4050+).
            Instead of switching to a heavy Electron IDE or copying snippets into a browser, you open a native Sublime side-by-side view
            where the assistant can work directly with your project.
          </p>
          <p>From a single conversation inside Sublime Text, you can ask Limitcode to:</p>
          <ul class="doc-bullets">
            <li><strong>Explore and explain your codebase:</strong> list folders, search for symbols or text across files, and read implementation details.</li>
            <li><strong>Write and refactor code:</strong> create new files, make targeted edits to existing functions, or apply multi-file patches.</li>
            <li><strong>Review every change safely:</strong> inspect proposed changes in Sublime's native diff view and undo or redo any edit in one keystroke (<kbd>Ctrl+Alt+U</kbd>).</li>
            <li><strong>Run commands and tests:</strong> execute your build scripts, test suites, linters, or git commands — and keep long-running dev servers running in the background.</li>
            <li><strong>Look up docs &amp; inspect screenshots:</strong> search the live web, fetch documentation pages, or analyze screenshots pasted directly into the chat (<kbd>Ctrl+V</kbd>).</li>
            <li><strong>Follow your team's workflow:</strong> automatically load repository rules (<code class="inline-code">AGENTS.md</code>), reusable <strong>Agent Skills</strong>, external <strong>MCP servers</strong>, and specialized <strong>Subagents</strong>.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <!-- SECTION 2: INSTALLATION & ACTIVATION -->
        <section id="installation" class="doc-block">
          <span class="doc-kicker">Getting Started</span>
          <h2>Installation &amp; License Activation</h2>
          <p>
            Limitcode Pro is distributed as a self-contained <code class="inline-code">.sublime-package</code> file. No external daemon or Python environment setup is required.
          </p>

          <div class="step-cards">
            <article class="step-card">
              <span class="step-badge">Step 1</span>
              <h3>Download your package</h3>
              <p>
                After purchasing on Gumroad, copy your license key from the receipt and download the <code class="inline-code">Limitcode.sublime-package</code> file matched to your Sublime Text 4 build.
              </p>
            </article>

            <article class="step-card">
              <span class="step-badge">Step 2</span>
              <h3>Place in Installed Packages</h3>
              <p>
                In Sublime Text, click <strong>Preferences → Browse Packages…</strong> (or <strong>Sublime Text → Settings → Browse Packages…</strong> on macOS).
                Go <strong>one folder up</strong> to open the <code class="inline-code">Installed Packages</code> directory, drop <code class="inline-code">Limitcode.sublime-package</code> inside, and restart Sublime Text.
              </p>
            </article>

            <article class="step-card">
              <span class="step-badge">Step 3</span>
              <h3>Activate your license</h3>
              <p>
                Open the Command Palette (<kbd>Ctrl+Shift+P</kbd> on Windows/Linux or <kbd>Cmd+Shift+P</kbd> on macOS), run <code class="inline-code">Limitcode Pro: Activate License</code>, and paste your license key.
              </p>
            </article>
          </div>

          <div class="callout">
            <strong>Managing your 3 devices &amp; working offline:</strong>
            Your license can be active on up to <strong>3 personal computers</strong> at the same time. You can check your seat status anytime with <code class="inline-code">Limitcode Pro: Show License Status</code>, or free up a seat to move to another computer using <code class="inline-code">Limitcode Pro: Deactivate This Device</code>. Once activated, you can work offline for up to 30 days between periodic checks.
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- SECTION 3: CONNECTING A PROVIDER -->
        <section id="connect-provider" class="doc-block">
          <span class="doc-kicker">Getting Started</span>
          <h2>Connecting an AI Provider</h2>
          <p>
            Limitcode supports over <strong>40 AI providers</strong>. You can connect models in four different ways depending on what you already use:
          </p>

          <div class="method-grid">
            <div class="method-card">
              <h3>1. Direct API Key (Fastest)</h3>
              <p>
                Press <kbd>Ctrl+Alt+K</kbd> or run <code class="inline-code">Limitcode: Setup Provider API Key</code>, choose your provider — such as <strong>OpenRouter</strong>, <strong>Anthropic</strong>, <strong>OpenAI</strong>, <strong>Google Gemini</strong>, <strong>DeepSeek</strong>, <strong>Mistral</strong>, <strong>Groq</strong>, or <strong>xAI</strong> — and paste your API key.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="method-card">
              <h3>2. Account Sign-In (OAuth)</h3>
              <p>
                Use an existing subscription without creating an API key. Run <code class="inline-code">Limitcode: Login with GitHub Copilot</code> with <kbd>Ctrl+Alt+B</kbd>, <code class="inline-code">Login with Codex</code> with <kbd>Ctrl+Alt+Z</kbd>, or <code class="inline-code">Login with Google Antigravity</code> with <kbd>Ctrl+Alt+Shift+G</kbd> and complete the browser sign-in.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="method-card">
              <h3>3. Existing CLI Login</h3>
              <p>
                If you already use <strong>Claude Code CLI</strong> or <strong>Codex CLI</strong> in your terminal and are logged in, Limitcode can reuse that session directly. Run <code class="inline-code">Limitcode: Login with Claude Code</code> with <kbd>Ctrl+Alt+E</kbd> or select the CLI provider.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="method-card">
              <h3>4. Local Models (Ollama &amp; LM Studio)</h3>
              <p>
                To run models 100% locally on your machine, start <strong>Ollama</strong> at <code class="inline-code">http://localhost:11434/v1</code>, <strong>LM Studio</strong> at <code class="inline-code">http://localhost:1234/v1</code>, or <strong>LiteLLM</strong>, then select it with <kbd>Ctrl+Alt+P</kbd> via <code class="inline-code">Limitcode: Change Provider</code>.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="sub-block" style="margin-top: 28px;">
            <h3>Complete List of Supported Providers</h3>
            <p>
              Below is the full catalogue of AI providers supported in Limitcode, along with each provider's Settings ID for <code class="inline-code">Limitcode.sublime-settings</code> and supported models:
            </p>

            <div class="provider-how-to">
              <div class="how-to-card">
                <span class="how-to-badge">Recommended</span>
                <h4 style="margin: 6px 0 4px; font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--ink);">Quick Setup via Command Palette</h4>
                <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.88rem; color: var(--ink-soft); line-height: 1.5;">
                  Press <kbd>Ctrl+Alt+K</kbd> (or <kbd>Cmd+Alt+K</kbd> on macOS) to run <em>Limitcode: Setup Provider API Key</em>, select your provider from the list, and paste your API key. Limitcode stores it securely in your settings.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="how-to-card">
                <span class="how-to-badge secondary">Manual JSON</span>
                <h4 style="margin: 6px 0 4px; font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--ink);">Configure in Sublime Settings</h4>
                <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.88rem; color: var(--ink-soft); line-height: 1.5;">
                  Open <code class="inline-code">Limitcode.sublime-settings</code> and add the provider's <strong>Settings ID</strong> under the <code class="inline-code">"api_keys"</code> object: <code class="inline-code">"api_keys": &#123; "&lt;settings_id&gt;": "your-key" &#125;</code>.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            {providerCatalog.map((group) => (
              <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
                <h4 style="margin: 0 0 6px; font-size: 1rem; color: var(--ink);">{group.category}</h4>
                <p style="margin: 0 0 12px; font-size: 0.88rem; color: var(--muted);">{group.subtitle}</p>
                <div class="table-wrap provider-table-wrap">
                  <table class="docs-table provider-table">
                    <colgroup>
                      <col style="width: 26%;" />
                      <col style="width: 18%;" />
                      <col style="width: 56%;" />
                    </colgroup>
                    <thead>
                      <tr>
                        <th>Provider</th>
                        <th>Settings ID</th>
                        <th>Supported Models &amp; Configuration</th>
                      </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                      {group.items.map((item) => (
                        <tr>
                          <td>
                            <div class="provider-name-cell">
                              <strong>{item.name}</strong>
                              {item.badge && <span class={`badge-pill badge-${item.badgeType}`}>{item.badge}</span>}
                            </div>
                          </td>
                          <td><code class="inline-code">{item.id}</code></td>
                          <td>
                            <div class="provider-desc">{item.description}</div>
                            {item.endpoint && (
                              <div class="provider-endpoint">
                                <span class="endpoint-label">Endpoint:</span>
                                <code class="endpoint-code">{item.endpoint}</code>
                              </div>
                            )}
                          </td>
                        </tr>
                      ))}
                    </tbody>
                  </table>
                </div>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>

          <div class="sub-block" style="margin-top: 24px;">
            <h3>Configuring Multiple Providers &amp; Custom Base URLs</h3>
            <p>
              You can configure as many providers at once as you like inside <code class="inline-code">Preferences → Package Settings → Limitcode → Settings</code>. When you open the unified model selector with <kbd>Ctrl+Alt+M</kbd>, Limitcode combines the available models from all your configured providers into a single list and automatically updates both <code class="inline-code">default_provider</code> and <code class="inline-code">default_model</code> when you pick one:
            </p>
            <pre><code>{providerSetupExample}</code></pre>
          </div>

          <p style="margin-top: 18px;">
            Want to compare billing, privacy, and account boundaries across connection methods? <a class="text-link" href="/providers/">Read the dedicated Providers Guide →</a>
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- SECTION 4: FIRST TASK -->
        <section id="first-task" class="doc-block">
          <span class="doc-kicker">Getting Started</span>
          <h2>Running Your First Task</h2>
          <ol class="numbered-steps">
            <li>
              <strong>Open the chat panel:</strong> Press <kbd>Ctrl+Alt+L</kbd> or run <code class="inline-code">Limitcode: Open Chat</code>. A native side-by-side chat view opens next to your code.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Pick your model:</strong> Press <kbd>Ctrl+Alt+M</kbd> or run <code class="inline-code">Limitcode: Change Model</code> to choose from all models available across your configured providers.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Ask a question or request an edit:</strong> Type a prompt such as <em>"Explain how authentication works in @src/auth.py and add unit tests for token expiry"</em> and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Approve or undo:</strong> When Limitcode proposes a file edit or shell command, approve it from the quick prompt. If you want to revert an edit at any point, press <kbd>Ctrl+Alt+U</kbd>.
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <!-- SECTION 5: USING THE CHAT -->
        <section id="chat-interface" class="doc-block">
          <span class="doc-kicker">Using the Chat</span>
          <h2>The Chat Panel &amp; Session Management</h2>
          <p>
            Limitcode's chat is a native Sublime Text view designed for keyboard-first navigation. Every conversation is automatically saved per project so you can close Sublime and pick up right where you left off.
          </p>

          <div class="feature-detail-list">
            <div class="feature-detail">
              <h3>Managing conversations</h3>
              <p>
                Start a fresh conversation anytime with <kbd>Ctrl+Alt+N</kbd> via <code class="inline-code">New Chat</code>. Browse and reopen previous sessions for your project with <kbd>Ctrl+Alt+H</kbd> via <code class="inline-code">Chat History</code>, give a session a memorable title with <kbd>Ctrl+Alt+Y</kbd> via <code class="inline-code">Rename Session</code>, or remove old chats with <kbd>Ctrl+Alt+D</kbd> via <code class="inline-code">Delete Session</code>.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="feature-detail">
              <h3>Prompt history &amp; cancelling runs</h3>
              <p>
                Inside the chat input box, press the <kbd>↑</kbd> and <kbd>↓</kbd> arrow keys to cycle through your previously sent prompts. If a response is taking too long or heading in the wrong direction, press <kbd>Ctrl+Alt+X</kbd> to cancel the active request, or press <kbd>Ctrl+Alt+Shift+X</kbd> or <kbd>Escape</kbd> to stop the agent immediately. The chat stays ready for your next instruction.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="feature-detail">
              <h3>Code blocks, live checklists &amp; status bar</h3>
              <p>
                Code snippets generated in the chat include quick actions to <strong>copy the block</strong>, <strong>insert it into your active editor</strong>, or <strong>create a new file</strong>. When the agent tackles a multi-step task, it displays a <strong>Live Task Checklist</strong> in the chat so you can follow its progress step by step. At the bottom of the window, the Sublime status bar always shows your active provider, model, work mode, reasoning effort, context usage, and session cost.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="feature-detail">
              <h3>Customizing font size &amp; model thoughts</h3>
              <p>
                Want the chat text slightly smaller or larger than your code editor? Run <code class="inline-code">Limitcode: Increase Chat Font Size</code>, <code class="inline-code">Decrease Chat Font Size</code>, or <code class="inline-code">Reset Chat Font Size</code> from the Command Palette. You can also show or hide the model's internal reasoning process anytime by pressing <kbd>Ctrl+Alt+T</kbd> or running <code class="inline-code">Toggle Show Thoughts</code>.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- SECTION 6: PASSING CONTEXT -->
        <section id="passing-context" class="doc-block">
          <span class="doc-kicker">Using the Chat</span>
          <h2>Passing Files &amp; Code Context</h2>
          <p>
            You never have to manually copy and paste code between your editor and the chat. Limitcode gives you three ways to point the agent at the exact code you care about:
          </p>

          <div class="sub-groups">
            <div class="sub-group">
              <h3>1. Mention files with <code class="inline-code">@</code></h3>
              <p>
                Type <code class="inline-code">@</code> directly inside the chat input to reference a file or path in your workspace, such as <code class="inline-code">@src/components/Navbar.tsx</code>. Limitcode attaches the file path so the agent knows exactly which file to inspect or modify.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="sub-group">
              <h3>2. Send Selection or Active File</h3>
              <p>
                Highlight a function, class, or block of lines in any open editor tab and press <kbd>Ctrl+Alt+A</kbd> or run <code class="inline-code">Limitcode: Send to Agent</code>. If nothing is highlighted, pressing <kbd>Ctrl+Alt+A</kbd> sends the entire active file. The code is attached to your chat prompt immediately.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="sub-group">
              <h3>3. Let the agent search on its own</h3>
              <p>
                Don't know which file handles a feature? Just ask in plain language — for example, <em>"Where do we validate webhook signatures?"</em> Limitcode will search your repository, list relevant directories, and read the matching files automatically.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="callout">
            <strong>Tip — Saved files vs. unsaved buffers:</strong>
            When Limitcode reads or searches files on its own, it reads the saved files on disk. If you have unsaved edits in an open tab that you want the agent to see right now, either save the file first with <kbd>Ctrl+S</kbd> or press <kbd>Ctrl+Alt+A</kbd> to send the live buffer.
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- SECTION 7: SCREENSHOTS & WEB SEARCH -->
        <section id="images-and-web" class="doc-block">
          <span class="doc-kicker">Using the Chat</span>
          <h2>Pasting Screenshots &amp; Configuring Web Search</h2>

          <div class="sub-groups">
            <div class="sub-group">
              <h3>1. Inspect images &amp; screenshots</h3>
              <p>
                Copy any screenshot, UI glitch, terminal error dialog, database diagram, or Figma mockup to your clipboard and press <kbd>Ctrl+V</kbd> or <kbd>Cmd+V</kbd> on macOS directly inside the chat. Vision-capable models such as Claude Sonnet 5, Claude Opus 5.5, GPT-6, and Gemini 4 will analyze the image alongside your code.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="sub-group">
              <h3>2. Built-in Web Search (Works out of the box, no setup required)</h3>
              <p>
                You can ask Limitcode to search the web for up-to-date documentation, library changelogs, or error messages — or ask it to fetch and read a specific URL. Both actions ask for your approval before making external requests.
              </p>
              <p style="margin-top: 10px;">
                <strong>Default zero-config search:</strong> Even if you haven't configured any search API key, Limitcode includes a built-in search that works out of the box using <strong>DuckDuckGo</strong> or the Chinese search engine <strong>Sogou</strong>. While convenient for quick lookups without setup, these free default engines are basic and not always the most accurate for deep technical queries.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="sub-group">
              <h3>3. How to configure Brave Search or Tavily (Recommended for best results)</h3>
              <p>
                For much higher quality and more reliable technical search results, configure <strong>Brave Search</strong> or <strong>Tavily</strong> inside your Limitcode settings by pressing <kbd>Ctrl+Alt+S</kbd> or opening <strong>Preferences → Package Settings → Limitcode → Settings</strong>, and adding the <code class="inline-code">"web_search"</code> block:
              </p>
              <pre class="codeblock codeblock-sm"><code>{webSearchExample}</code></pre>
              <ul class="doc-bullets" style="margin-top: 14px;">
                <li><strong><code class="inline-code">"provider": "auto"</code> (Default):</strong> Automatically uses <strong>Brave</strong> or <strong>Tavily</strong> as soon as you paste a <code class="inline-code">brave_api_key</code> or <code class="inline-code">tavily_api_key</code>. If both keys are empty, it falls back to the built-in zero-config search with <code class="inline-code">duckduckgo</code> or <code class="inline-code">sogou</code>.</li>
                <li><strong>Force a specific search engine:</strong> Change <code class="inline-code">"provider"</code> to <code class="inline-code">"brave"</code>, <code class="inline-code">"tavily"</code>, <code class="inline-code">"duckduckgo"</code>, or <code class="inline-code">"sogou"</code>.</li>
                <li><strong><code class="inline-code">"max_results"</code>:</strong> Controls how many search results are returned to the agent per query. The default is <code class="inline-code">5</code>.</li>
                <li><strong>Provider-native web search:</strong> Some AI providers, such as Anthropic Claude and Moonshot / Kimi, also offer their own built-in server-side web search, which Limitcode can use automatically when supported by the active model.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- SECTION 8: EDITING FILES -->
        <section id="editing-files" class="doc-block">
          <span class="doc-kicker">Working with Code</span>
          <h2>Creating, Editing &amp; Refactoring Files</h2>
          <p>
            In <strong>Code</strong> or <strong>Debug</strong> mode, Limitcode can make changes directly to your project files once you approve them:
          </p>
          <ul class="doc-bullets">
            <li><strong>Targeted edits:</strong> Update specific functions, imports, or classes inside large files without rewriting the rest of the file.</li>
            <li><strong>Multi-file patches:</strong> Apply coordinated diffs across several files during a refactor or feature rollout.</li>
            <li><strong>New file scaffolding:</strong> Create new modules, components, tests, or configuration files in your project tree.</li>
            <li><strong>Automatic LSP error self-correction:</strong> If you use Sublime Text's <strong>LSP</strong> package, Limitcode checks for fresh syntax and type diagnostics right after editing a file. If the edit introduced a type error or missing import, those diagnostics are fed back to the agent automatically so it can fix the issue on the spot.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <!-- SECTION 9: DIFFS & UNDO -->
        <section id="diffs-and-undo" class="doc-block">
          <span class="doc-kicker">Working with Code</span>
          <h2>Native Diff Review, Undo &amp; Snapshot History</h2>
          <p>
            You never have to worry about an AI agent silently breaking your working tree. Limitcode gives you three layers of edit safety:
          </p>
          <div class="sub-groups">
            <div class="sub-group">
              <h3>1. Native Sublime diff view</h3>
              <p>
